<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles D. B. King</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Charles Dunbar Burgess King</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles D. B. King was the 17th president of Liberia, in office from 1920 to 1930. As secretary of state he represented Liberia at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> of 1919, and as president he granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firestone-tire-and-rubber-company">Firestone</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> concession of 1926. When a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> commission found in 1930 that senior officials had been involved in shipping forced labourers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Fernando Po</a>, he resigned with his vice-president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-d-b-king.jpg" alt="Charles D. B. King" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Auguste Léon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1927_Charles_Dunbar_Burgess_King.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Dunbar Burgess King, born on 12 March 1875, was of Americo-Liberian and Sierra Leone Creole descent. He belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-whig-party">True Whig Party</a>, which ruled Liberia from 1878 until 1980. He was Attorney General from 1904 until 1912 and Secretary of State from 1912 until his election as president in 1919. In that capacity he attended the 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> and the First Pan-African Congress that accompanied it, and Liberia had become a charter member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1919. King was a moderate supporter of reform who continued to support the patronage machine of the True Whig Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>King became president in 1920 and served for ten years. In 1929 he helped to found the Booker Washington Institute in Kakata. Liberia's finances were in crisis, and in March 1921 he led a commission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to seek reorganisation of the country's debt. The State Department favoured a loan of 5 million dollars, and the House of Representatives approved it by 148 votes to 139, but the Senate rejected it by 42 to 32, to the disappointment of Liberian officials, who feared that British and French designs on their country might now prove unstoppable. The Firestone Rubber Company later agreed with his government on a 99-year lease of 1,000,000 acres for rubber growing, on which the Liberian economy came to depend.</p><p>King's challenger in the 1927 election, Thomas J. R. Faulkner, lost to him by an official count of 234,000 votes in a country with 15,000 registered voters, and King was later listed in the Guinness Book of World Records for the most fraudulent reported election in history. Faulkner then accused members of the government of recruiting contract labour and selling it as slaves.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> sent a commission under the British zoologist Cuthbert Christy, and President Herbert Hoover briefly suspended relations to press Liberia to comply. The Christy Report of 1930 supported many of the allegations and implicated officials including Vice President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allen-yancy">Allen Yancy</a>, who it found had approved forced labour for public works such as roads and for the shipment of goods abroad. King, Yancy and other implicated leaders resigned. His administration has been described as marked by patronage, nepotism and corruption. From 1947 to 1952 King served as Liberia's minister, and then ambassador, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles D. B. King” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1930 a League of Nations commission found that officials had shipped labourers to the Spanish island of Fernando Po under conditions resembling slavery, and President <b>Charles D. B. King</b> and Vice-President Allen Yancy resigned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/liberia">Liberia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/liberia">Liberia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.31,-10.81&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Monrovia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles D. B. King"><image href="/maps/globes/g00000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4087 4461h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4087 4461h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.31,-10.81&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
